## `[ExpectedException]` → `Assert.ThrowsExactly` in MSTest v4

The `ExpectedExceptionAttribute` was removed in v4 (deprecated since v3 via analyzer `MSTEST0006`). Replace it by moving the exception assertion **into the test body**:

**Synchronous:**
```csharp
// Before (v3)
[ExpectedException(typeof(InvalidOperationException))]
[TestMethod]
public void MyTest()
{
    service.DoWork();
}

// After (v4)
[TestMethod]
public void MyTest()
{
    Assert.ThrowsExactly<InvalidOperationException>(() => service.DoWork());
}
```

**Async:**
```csharp
// Before (v3)
[ExpectedException(typeof(HttpRequestException))]
[TestMethod]
public async Task MyAsyncTest()
{
    await client.GetAsync("https://localhost:0");
}

// After (v4)
[TestMethod]
public async Task MyAsyncTest()
{
    await Assert.ThrowsExactlyAsync<HttpRequestException>(
        () => client.GetAsync("https://localhost:0"));
}
```

**Key notes:**
- Wrap only the **throwing call** in the lambda — keep setup code outside.
- If the old attribute used `AllowDerivedTypes = true`, use `Assert.Throws<T>` / `Assert.ThrowsAsync<T>` instead (matches derived types too).
- `Assert.ThrowsExactly` requires an **exact** type match (same semantics as the old `[ExpectedException]` without `AllowDerivedTypes`).

